Vendored copy of the standard library TLS client.

Zig version: 0.16.0
Source path: /nix/store/afbxfxrx3fmvhzp7ni9li5l3v2fbw7yn-zig-0.16.0/lib/zig/std/crypto/tls/Client.zig
Line count at copy time: 1670

Why this file is here
---------------------
The standard library client cannot connect to a server that has an ECDSA
certificate and speaks only TLS 1.2. Measured against curl 8.21.0:

    ecc256.badssl.com     ECDSA, TLS 1.2   std fails, curl works
    ecc384.badssl.com     ECDSA, TLS 1.2   std fails, curl works
    tls-v1-2.badssl.com   RSA,   TLS 1.2   both work
    cloudflare.com        ECDSA, TLS 1.3   both work

There are two causes, one behind the other.

The first is the suite list at the end of the file. Every TLS 1.2 suite in it
is an `ECDHE_RSA` suite, and there is no `ECDHE_ECDSA` suite. A server that
holds only an ECDSA certificate and offers only TLS 1.2 therefore shares no
suite with the client, and it sends a `handshake_failure` alert. The TLS 1.3
suites name no signature algorithm, which is why the same server works over
TLS 1.3.

The second is in `CertificatePublicKey.verifySignature`. TLS 1.2 codes an
ECDSA signature as a hash and a signature algorithm, and the two are free of
each other. RFC 5246 section 7.4.1.4.1 and RFC 8422 section 5.1.3 say so. TLS
1.3 gave the same code points names that fix the curve as well, and RFC 8446
section 4.2.3 requires the curve of the key to be the curve in the name.
Upstream reads the TLS 1.3 meaning always. So a TLS 1.2 server that holds a
P-256 key and signs the Server Key Exchange with SHA-384 makes upstream parse
a P-256 point as a P-384 point, and it reports `InvalidEncoding`.
`ecc256.badssl.com` does exactly that:

    openssl s_client -connect ecc256.badssl.com:443 \
        -servername ecc256.badssl.com -sigalgs 'ECDSA+SHA256:ECDSA+SHA384'
    Peer signature type: ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384
    Server public key is 256 bit

Fixing only the suite list therefore moves `ecc256.badssl.com` from an alert
to `InvalidEncoding` and leaves it broken.

Only this one file is vendored. Everything the patch needs from
`std/crypto/tls.zig` and `std/crypto/Certificate.zig` is already there and
public:

* `tls.CipherSuite` already holds the six `ECDHE_ECDSA` values.
* `tls.CipherSuite.with` already maps each of them to the same record cipher
  as its `ECDHE_RSA` twin.
* The Client Hello already advertises `ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256` and
  `ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384` in `signature_algorithms`.
* `Certificate.Parsed.PubKeyAlgo` already carries the named curve of an EC
  key, and the call site in `init` already passes that union. Upstream throws
  the curve away at the field.
* `Certificate.NamedCurve.Curve` already gives the P-256 and P-384 types, and
  `crypto.sign.ecdsa.Ecdsa` already takes a curve and a hash apart.
* `std.crypto.Certificate.verify` already handles `ecdsa_with_SHA256` and
  `ecdsa_with_SHA384`, so the chain check needs nothing.

So `tls.zig` and `Certificate.zig` stay upstream.

How to re-sync
--------------
1. Copy the new upstream `Client.zig` over this file.
2. Change `@import("../../std.zig")` to `@import("std")`, and keep the
   `ZURL PATCH` comment that sits on that line.
3. Reapply the patch below, item by item. Every patched line carries a
   `ZURL PATCH` comment, so `grep -n 'ZURL PATCH' Client.zig` finds all of
   it. A comment that runs over several lines carries one on its first line,
   and the block at the end of the file carries one on the line that opens
   it.

   **Work from the numbered list, not from the guard.** `check-vendor`
   reports a change nobody wrote down. It cannot report a patch that got
   lost, because a lost patch leaves this file agreeing with upstream. So a
   re-sync that skips item 13 reverts the host check and the guard still
   says the copy is clean. The list is the only record of what must come
   back.
4. Run `zig build test --summary all`.
5. Run `zig build check-vendor`. It compares this file with the same file in
   the Zig that the flake gives, and it fails on a changed line that no marker
   accounts for. It reads the library directory of that Zig while it runs, so
   it needs no edit when Zig changes.

   The guard counts the upstream lines a hunk drops against the lines of
   **code** the hunk puts back with a marker. A comment line pays for
   nothing, because a comment runs nothing. So a patch that replaces two
   upstream lines needs two marked lines of code, however much comment it
   also carries.
6. Update the version, the path, and the line count above.

The second reason this file is here
-----------------------------------
Upstream sends no ALPN extension at all. RFC 7301 makes that extension the
only way a client and a server agree on the protocol above TLS, so a server
that would offer HTTP/2 never gets the chance to say so, and curl needs the
chance. `zurl-net/Connection.zig` offers `h2` and then `http/1.1`, and
`zurl-http/h2.zig` speaks the answer.

Nothing else in `std` has to move for this. `tls.ExtensionType` already holds
`application_layer_protocol_negotiation`, and `tls.Decoder` already bounds a
sub-decoder at a length the peer wrote.

The third reason this file is here
----------------------------------
Upstream cannot verify a certificate for an address, and it walks a subject
alternative name block with no bound.

`Certificate.Parsed.verifyHostName` reads a `dNSName` and skips every other
kind of general name. A certificate for an address carries an `iPAddress`
name, tag 7, so that walk matches none. `https://1.1.1.1/` and
`https://8.8.8.8/` failed with `error.CertificateHostMismatch`, and curl
8.21.0 fetches both. Every IPv6 literal over `https` failed the same way.

The same walk trusts every length the certificate wrote. `der.Element.parse`
reads two octets past an index with no bound, reads each long form length
octet with no bound, and adds the length to the index in `u32`, which can
pass the end of the block and can wrap. A leaf certificate holding the six
bytes `30 20 82 1E 61 62` panicked the client with "index out of bounds:
index 34, len 6". `init` runs the host check on the leaf before any trust
check, so those six bytes crashed the client before authentication.

The fifth reason this file is here
----------------------------------
Upstream accepts a forged certificate for any host, signed by any
certificate that chains to a trusted root.

`Certificate.Parsed.verify` checks three things and no more: that the
subject's issuer name equals the issuer's subject name, that the subject is
inside its validity dates, and that the signature holds. It reads neither
`basicConstraints` nor `keyUsage`. `Certificate.zig` names the object
identifier of both extensions in `ExtensionId` and it consults neither
during verification. `Bundle.verify` calls the same function. So upstream
treats every certificate in a chain as able to issue.

An attacker buys a free domain validated certificate for a name they own.
They sign a certificate for a name they do not own with that leaf's private
key, and they send the two together:

    cert[0]  leaf, subject alternative name = victim.test,
             issuer = attacker.test, signed with the attacker's leaf key
    cert[1]  the real attacker.test leaf, issued by a real intermediate
    cert[2]  the real intermediate

The host walk passes on cert[0], the leaf key of cert[1] checks the
signature on cert[0] because the attacker made it, and the trust store
finds the real root for cert[2]. The CertificateVerify message is then
checked under a key the attacker holds. That is a machine in the middle of
every HTTPS transfer, and of every HTTP/3 transfer, for the price of one
domain validated certificate.

RFC 5280 gives three rules that close it:

* Section 4.2.1.9: a certificate whose key checks a certificate signature
  must carry `basicConstraints` with `cA` TRUE.
* Section 4.2.1.3: if that certificate carries `keyUsage`, the extension
  must assert `keyCertSign`.
* Section 4.2.1.9: `pathLenConstraint` counts the certificates that may sit
  between the issuer and the leaf.

`verifyIssued` in the appended block holds all three, and it is the only
place they live. See item 17.

The patch
---------
There are nineteen places, and three of them are inside the appended block
of item 14, so those change no upstream line. The first of them belongs to
the copy itself rather than to ECDSA:

0. The import at the top of the file. This copy does not sit inside the
   standard library, so it reads `@import("std")` where upstream reads a
   relative path.

The suite list, which fixes the alert:

1. The doc comment above `cipher_suites`, which says why the list holds two
   forms of each TLS 1.2 suite.

2. Six lines in `cipher_suites`, three in each arm of the `if`. Each one adds
   the `ECDHE_ECDSA` form beside the `ECDHE_RSA` form that upstream has. The
   ECDSA form comes first in each pair, which agrees with the order that curl
   and OpenSSL send, so a server that holds both certificates gives the faster
   one.

3. Three lines in the Server Hello switch that builds the handshake cipher.
   `CipherSuite.with` maps the ECDSA form and the RSA form to the same record
   cipher, so the body of the arm holds for either one.

4. Three lines in the `tls_1_2` switch that moves the handshake on to the
   certificate. The key exchange is ECDHE for both forms.

The signature check, which fixes `InvalidEncoding`:

5. The `algo` field of `CertificatePublicKey` and the parameter of its `init`.
   Both hold `Certificate.Parsed.PubKeyAlgo` in place of the tag
   `Certificate.AlgorithmCategory`, so the named curve of an EC key travels
   with the key. The call site in `init` passes that union already, so it does
   not change. The one comparison that read the field as a tag now takes the
   tag out of the union.

6. Four members of `VerifyError`, for the four combinations of curve and hash
   that the check can now build. Upstream lists only the two that a TLS 1.3
   scheme name allows.

7. One parameter of `verifySignature`, `tls_version`, and the two call sites
   that pass it. The version is what decides whether the curve of the scheme
   name is binding.

8. The ECDSA arm of `verifySignature`, which now calls `verifyEcdsa` in the
   appended block.

The version ceiling, which gives curl's `--tls-max` an answer:

9. `Options.max_version`, a `grease_version` constant, and one block in
   `init` after `cleartext_header_buf`. The block runs only for a ceiling of
   TLS 1.2. It writes the GREASE version RFC 8701 reserves over the TLS 1.3
   entry of `supported_versions`, so the list still holds two entries and
   every length in the hello stays what upstream computed. A server must
   ignore a GREASE value, so the hello then offers TLS 1.2 alone. The block
   reads the two bytes back with `std.debug.assert` before it writes them.
   That assert is compiled out in ReleaseFast, so it is a check for a
   developer and not a guarantee for a user. The wire byte test in the
   appended block is what holds the offset in place in every build.
   `cleartext_header_buf` becomes a `var` for this, and nothing else about it
   changes.

ALPN, which is the precondition for HTTP/2:

10. `Options.alpn_protocols`, two members of `InitError`, and the `alpn`
   field of `Client`. The option is the list of protocol names the hello
   offers, in the order of preference, and an empty list sends no extension,
   which is curl's `--no-alpn`. `TlsAlpnOfferInvalid` answers a list with no
   wire form, and `TlsAlpnProtocolNotOffered` answers a peer that chose a
   name the hello did not carry. The field holds the answer by value,
   because a `Client` is copied into whatever owns the session.

11. Five places in `init`, and one line each.

   The extension is built after `key_share`, into a stack buffer, by
   `encodeAlpn` in the appended block. It cannot go inside
   `cleartext_header_buf`: that buffer is built at compile time from `++`,
   and the list is a run-time length. So the bytes travel the way the host
   name already travels, as another slice of the `writeVecAll` call, and the
   `iovecs` array grows from two entries to three.

   Three lengths then have to count it, and each takes the one added term
   `+ alpn_extension.len`: the length of the extensions block, the `u24`
   length of the handshake message, and the `u16` length of the record.
   Each of the three already carried `- server_name_extension.len +
   server_name_extension_len` for the host, which is the same arithmetic for
   the same reason.

   The transcript hash takes the extension as well, as "Client Hello part
   3", because the hash covers every byte of the hello that went out.

   The answer is read in two places, because the two versions carry it in
   different messages. TLS 1.2 answers in the Server Hello, and the arm
   there puts the name in `alpn_hello`. That record is not authenticated
   when it is read, and a TLS 1.3 server answers in EncryptedExtensions
   instead, so `alpn_hello` reaches `alpn_selected` only where the
   negotiated version is TLS 1.2. TLS 1.3 answers in the
   `encrypted_extensions` arm.

   Upstream's `encrypted_extensions` arm reads the extension into `extd` and
   drops it with `_ = extd;`. The patch replaces that one discard line with
   `var alpn_extd = extd;`, and the ALPN arm consumes the copy. So the line
   above it stays exactly as upstream wrote it, and one marked line stands
   for one removed line.

The version floor and the downgrade sentinel:

12. One term in the `tls_1_2` arm of the Server Hello version switch:
   `options.max_version != .tls_1_2 and`. RFC 8446 section 4.1.3 asks a
   client that **offered** TLS 1.3 to abort on the "DOWNGRD" sentinel,
   because the sentinel then means an attacker took TLS 1.3 out of the
   hello. A client that offered TLS 1.2 alone, which is `--tls-max 1.2`,
   asked for exactly what it got, and a server that speaks TLS 1.3 sets the
   sentinel anyway. Measured: `--tls-max 1.2` against cloudflare.com aborted
   here, where curl runs. Without item 9 this term must go, because a hello
   that always offers TLS 1.3 must always abort on the sentinel.

The host check, which fixes an address and closes a panic:

13. One line in the `certificate` arm of `init`: the `.explicit` case calls
   `verifyHost` in the appended block, where upstream calls
   `subject.verifyHostName(host)`. That call runs on the leaf certificate,
   before the chain check, so everything it reads is bytes any server can
   send. See item 14 for what it calls.

The chain rules, which close the forged leaf:

16. One line in the `certificate` arm of `init`, where the walk checks a
   certificate against the one before it. Upstream reads
   `try prev_cert.verify(subject, now_sec);`. The copy reads
   `try verifyIssued(prev_cert, subject, chain_host, cert_index - 1,
   now_sec);`, which is the same check with the RFC 5280 rules in front of
   it. See item 17.

   One more line in the same arm, where the walk reads the leaf:
   `try verifyEndEntity(subject, trust_checked);`. A server whose leaf was
   signed by a root of the trust store sends one certificate, so
   `verifyIssued` never runs for that chain and the rules a leaf must obey
   on its own need a call of their own. See item 17.

   Two lines before the loop that read `chain_host` and `trust_checked`
   out of `Options`. `chain_host` is the host of the session, which every
   `nameConstraints` in the chain must allow. `trust_checked` says whether
   the caller asked for a trust check at all.

   `InitError` grows by `|| IssuerError` on the line that closes the set,
   so the three new names reach every caller. That line carries a marker
   and the comment above it says why.

   One line in `CertificatePublicKey.init`. Upstream refuses a
   `subjectPublicKey` that is too long and takes one that is empty. A
   BIT STRING of `03 01 00` parses cleanly and gives a key of no bytes,
   which reached an assert in Debug and a signature check in ReleaseFast.
   The line now refuses `pub_key.len == 0` as well.

The appended block:

14. A block at the end of the file, after a marker comment. It holds
   `CertificateEcdsa`, `schemeCurve` and `verifyEcdsa`, which are the ECDSA
   check that item 8 calls. It holds `alpn_max_protocol_len`,
   `alpn_max_list_len`, `alpn_extension_max`, `AlpnSelection`,
   `alpnProtocol`, `encodeAlpn` and `readAlpn`, which are the ALPN parts
   that item 11 calls.

   It holds `WriteLock`, which is the lock of item 19.

   It holds `WantedName`, `verifyHost`, `parseName`, `walkSubjectAltNames`
   and `checkHostName`, which are the host check that item 13 calls.
   `verifyHost` reads the host as an address and, on either answer, calls
   the one walk. `walkSubjectAltNames` is that walk, and it reads a
   `dNSName` for a name and an `iPAddress` for an address. `parseName` is
   the only way the walk reads a DER element, and it is where every bound
   lives: the two octets of the header, each long form length octet, and the
   end of the value, all against the length of the block, all in `u64` so no
   sum can wrap. **A third kind of host must go through `parseName` as
   well.** There is no second walk to write one in, which is the point of
   the shape.

   `checkHostName` is the one part of the block that is upstream text.
   `Certificate.Parsed.checkHostName` is private to `std/crypto/Certificate.zig`,
   so the walk cannot call it. Compare the two at each Zig bump: a change to
   the wildcard rule upstream must be copied here.

   The block also holds the tests that keep the whole patch in place. The
   tests read the offered suite list, they drive `init` with a Server Hello
   that the block itself builds, they read back the bytes of one Client
   Hello and check every length in it, they run the host check over subject
   alternative name blocks the block itself builds, truncated ones included,
   and they sign and verify with keys that the block itself makes. No test
   in it opens a socket.

   `encodeAlpn` writes the two outer lengths last, from the position the
   names reached, so neither one can name a count that the bytes do not
   hold. `readAlpn` reads the peer's answer inside a decoder that covers
   the extension and nothing else, it refuses a list that is not exactly
   one name, and it compares that name against the offered list. A name
   outside the list is `TlsAlpnProtocolNotOffered` and the handshake stops,
   because a session carrying a protocol zurl cannot parse is worse than no
   session. A peer that sends no extension is not a fault: RFC 7301 lets a
   server that shares no protocol leave it out, and `alpnProtocol` then
   gives null.

The fourth reason this file is here
-----------------------------------
QUIC carries the TLS 1.3 handshake in CRYPTO frames and not in TLS
records, and it asks TLS for a secret at each of three encryption levels.
RFC 9001 sections 4 and 5. `init` above can give neither: it reads records
and it produces a record cipher.

The package `zurl-quic-tls` runs that handshake instead. **It holds no
certificate code at all.** The chain walk, the host name walk, the address
walk, the trust store, the CertificateVerify check, the Diffie-Hellman,
and the ALPN offer are all in this file, and that package calls them. So a
trust rule added here reaches HTTP/3 on the day it lands, and QUIC gets no
second path with weaker checks.

15. A namespace named `quic`, in the appended block, which is the whole of
   that door. Nothing outside the block changed for it, so `check-vendor`
   sees no new hunk and a re-sync needs no extra step.

   `KeyExchange` wraps `KeyShare`, so both transports generate the same
   key pairs from the same 176 byte seed and run the same scalar
   multiplication. `writeShare` writes one `KeyShareEntry`, and it refuses
   a group outside `key_share_groups`.

   `CertificateKey` wraps `CertificatePublicKey`, so the ECDSA curve rule
   of item 8, the RSA modulus widths, and the Ed25519 arm are the ones
   `init` uses. It passes `.tls_1_3` always, because RFC 9001 section 4.2
   gives QUIC no other version, so the curve rule stays the strict one.

   `verifyCertificate` runs the whole certificate check of one TLS 1.3
   Certificate message: `verifyHost` on the leaf, `verifyIssued` between
   neighbours, and `Options.ca` for the trust root, in the order the
   `certificate` arm of `init` uses. **It is a second walk of the DER list
   and not a second set of rules**, and item 17 is what makes that
   sentence true instead of hopeful. It could not call into `init`
   instead: replacing that arm's eighty lines with one call would drop
   more upstream lines than a marked line puts back, which the guard
   refuses. `host` and `ca` are `@FieldType(Options, ...)`, so a change to
   either field's shape reaches this call at compile time.

   `encodeAlpnExtension` and `readAlpnAnswer` are `encodeAlpn` and
   `readAlpn`, so a QUIC hello and a TLS hello carry the same offer and
   check the same answer. The one difference belongs to the caller: RFC
   9001 section 8.1 makes ALPN mandatory for QUIC, so the QUIC handshake
   reports a missing extension as a fault where a TLS session accepts it.

17. `max_chain_certificates`, `IssuerRights`, `IssuerError`,
   `ExtensionError`, `oid_basic_constraints`, `oid_key_usage`,
   `oid_subject_alt_name`, `oid_name_constraints`, `oid_ext_key_usage`,
   `oid_any_ext_key_usage`, `oid_server_auth`, `issuerRights`,
   `readBasicConstraints`, `readKeyUsage`, `readExtKeyUsage`,
   `checkNameConstraints`, `SubtreeMatch`, `walkSubtrees`, `dnsInSubtree`,
   `addressInSubtree`, `verifyEndEntity` and `verifyIssued`, in the
   appended block. They are the chain rules of the fifth reason above.

   **An extension marked critical that this file does not read refuses the
   certificate.** RFC 5280 section 4.2 and section 6.1.4 (f) say so, and
   `issuerRights` holds the rule for every certificate of a chain except
   the trust anchor. It is what keeps this client closed when an extension
   nobody has written yet arrives, and it is why `nameConstraints`,
   `policyConstraints` and `inhibitAnyPolicy` can no longer be dropped in
   silence.

   **`nameConstraints` binds the host of the session.** RFC 5280 section
   4.2.1.10 lets an authority bind the authorities below it to a name
   space, and the CA/Browser Forum Baseline Requirements let such a
   sub-authority go without an audit because clients hold it to that name
   space. `checkNameConstraints` reads the host the user asked for, which
   is the one name the session authenticates, and refuses a chain that may
   not answer for it. A `dNSName` subtree and an `iPAddress` subtree name
   a TLS server; a subtree of any other kind is refused, because this side
   cannot prove the certificate obeys it.

   **`extendedKeyUsage` must name server authentication.** RFC 5280
   section 4.2.1.12: a certificate that carries the extension may be used
   for the purposes it names and for no other, so a key bound to
   electronic mail or to code signing stands behind no TLS server. Both a
   leaf and a certificate authority answer this rule.

   **`verifyIssued` is the one place the chain rules live.** Both walks of
   a certificate list call it: the `certificate` arm of `init` for TLS
   over TCP, and `quic.verifyCertificate` for the handshake QUIC carries
   in CRYPTO frames. Neither one calls `Certificate.Parsed.verify` for a
   neighbour any more, so a rule added to `verifyIssued` reaches both
   transports on the day it lands. A test named "both certificate walks
   call the one chain rule" reads this file's own source and holds that
   shape in place.

   The walk cannot be collapsed further than that. The two loops read
   different bytes: a TLS 1.2 Certificate message carries no request
   context and no per-certificate extensions, and `init` also builds a
   `Certificate.Chain` and moves `handshake_state`. Replacing the
   thirty-six upstream lines of that arm with one call would drop more
   upstream lines than marked lines put back, and the guard refuses that.
   So the **rules** are one function and the **byte reader** is two loops,
   and the drift the guard cannot see is the drift that no longer
   matters.

   `issuerRights` reads the `[3] EXPLICIT Extensions` field of the
   `tbsCertificate`. Every element it reads goes through `parseName`, the
   one bounded reader of item 14, so a length the peer wrote cannot pass
   the end of the buffer and cannot wrap. It walks the fields of the
   `tbsCertificate` in order and stops at the one tagged `[3]`, so no
   earlier field can be read as the extensions block. A certificate below
   version 3 carries no extensions and gets the default answer, which says
   it is not a certificate authority.

18. The tests for item 17, in the appended block. They mint real X.509
   version 3 certificates with a DER writer that works from the inside
   out, they sign them with real P-256 keys, and they run them through
   `verifyIssued` and through `quic.verifyCertificate` with a real
   `Certificate.Bundle`. The attack test builds the three certificate
   chain of the fifth reason and asserts `CertificateIssuerNotCa`. Its
   control builds a chain that follows RFC 5280 and asserts that the same
   walk takes it. No test reads a file and no test opens a socket.

The sixth reason this file is here
----------------------------------
Upstream couples the read path to the write path, so one session cannot be
driven by two tasks.

Everything else about the two paths is independent. The read path decrypts
with `server_key` and counts with `read_seq`, the write path encrypts with
`client_key` and counts with `write_seq`, and `input` and `output` are two
buffers over one socket. The one exception is a TLS 1.3 `key_update` of kind
`update_requested`: RFC 8446 section 4.6.3 makes a peer that asks for one
expect the other side to rotate its own write keys, so that arm of
`readIndirect` writes `client_secret`, `client_key`, `client_iv`, and
`write_seq`. A task that was encrypting a record at that moment would use
half of one key and half of another, and a `write_seq` that went back to
zero under an unchanged key would repeat a nonce.

`zurl-http/h2.zig` needs the two paths to run at once. An HTTP/2 connection
that several transfers share has one task inside the frame read while
another writes a request, and a design that let the reader hold a lock over
the read would serialise the transfers it exists to overlap. That is the
finding `p4-h2-complete-report.md` recorded, and this is what closes it.

19. One field, `write_lock`, and two pairs of lines.

   The field holds a lock the owner of the session gives, or null. A
   `Client` cannot name a lock of its own: `std.Io.Mutex` needs the
   `std.Io` of the caller and a `Client` holds none, and a
   `std.Thread.Mutex` would stop a whole thread of an event loop that runs
   several tasks on one thread. So `WriteLock` in the appended block is a
   two-call vtable, and `zurl-net/Connection.shareBetweenTasks` fills it
   with the connection's own `std.Io.Mutex`.

   The `.update_requested` arm of `readIndirect` takes the lock around the
   rotation, and `prepareCiphertextRecord` takes it for as long as it
   encrypts. Those are the only two places that touch the write keys or
   `write_seq`.

   **No upstream line is dropped.** All four lines are added, so the hunks
   cost nothing against the guard's count, and a session with no lock runs
   one compare and no lock at all: the behaviour of a `Client` that nobody
   shares is byte for byte upstream's.

   **No syscall runs under the lock.** `drain`, `flush`, and `end` each ask
   `output` for room before they call `prepareCiphertextRecord`, so the
   flush that a full socket buffer needs has already happened. What is
   left under the lock is the AEAD, which is arithmetic. So a reader that
   waits for the lock waits for one record's encryption and never for a
   peer.

   **What this does not fix.** Upstream rotates its write keys on an
   `update_requested` and sends no `KeyUpdate` message of its own, which
   RFC 8446 section 4.6.3 requires. The peer therefore never learns when
   to change its read keys. That is an upstream defect and this patch
   keeps it exactly as it is: the rotation happens at the same record it
   always happened at, and only the task that performs it changed.

20. Four lines that bound a length the peer wrote, one in `init` and three
   in `readIndirect`. Each one stands in front of a subtraction that
   upstream leaves open, and each one refuses an input that made the
   subtraction wrap.

   `readIndirect` reads the two octet length of the record header and
   checks it against `max_ciphertext_len` alone, which bounds it from
   above and not from below. The TLS 1.3 arm then subtracts the tag
   length and the TLS 1.2 arm subtracts the explicit IV and the tag, both
   in `u16`. The five octets `17 03 03 00 00` from the server therefore
   gave a length of 65520, the `take` of that length failed into a `catch
   unreachable`, and the record cipher wrote into a slice whose length the
   optimiser was free to choose. The same function then strips the zero
   padding of a TLS 1.3 record and reads the inner content type at
   `msg.len - 1`, which wraps for a plaintext of all zero octets. RFC 8446
   section 5.4 makes such a record one to refuse.

   The handshake loop of `init` has the third shape of the same fault: it
   adds the length of the stripped plaintext to `cleartext_fragment_end`
   and then steps back one octet to the inner content type. A record that
   decrypts to no octets left nothing to step back over.

   Upstream's own handshake loop carries the two lower bounds on
   `record_len` that `readIndirect` does not, so this is an omission
   upstream made and not a rule this side invented. The tests are in
   `test_server.zig`, under `Options.fault`, and each one drives the
   shipped client against a server that sends the record.

21. One line in `init` that bounds the host before it is cast, one line in
   `verifySignature` that refuses `rsa_pkcs1_sha1`, one member dropped from
   the offered `signature_algorithms`, and one modulus width dropped from
   the RSA arm of `verifySignature`. The cryptographic floor.

   Upstream offers `rsa_pkcs1_sha1` in every client hello and accepts it in
   `CertificateVerify`. RFC 8446 section 4.4.3 forbids it there outright
   and RFC 9155 deprecates SHA-1 in every TLS signature. Measured on the
   wire: curl 8.21.0 with OpenSSL 3.6.3 offers 26 signature schemes and not
   one of them is SHA-1. zurl now offers twelve and none is SHA-1 either.
   The two `switch` prongs for the scheme stay as upstream wrote them, and
   the line in front of them makes both unreachable, so the hunk is one
   added line and not two removals.

   Upstream also accepts a 128 octet RSA modulus, which is 1024 bits. The
   CA/Browser Forum Baseline Requirements section 6.1.5 and NIST SP 800-57
   Part 1 Revision 5 both put the floor at 2048 bits, and no public
   authority has issued a shorter key for many years.

   `host_len` was an `@intCast` of a count that reaches this file from a
   url, and a url reaches zurl from a `Location` header. An `@intCast` that
   does not fit panics a checked build and is undefined behaviour in the
   ReleaseFast build a user runs, so the bound is a named refusal in front
   of it. See `host_name_max`.

22. Two lines in the `key_update` arm of `readIndirect`, and one line in
   front of the sub-message loop above it.

   The arm reads `p.tls_1_3` out of `tls.ApplicationCipher`, whose payload
   is an **untagged** union, and it never looks at the negotiated version.
   On a TLS 1.2 session it therefore read the live `tls_1_2` bytes as a TLS
   1.3 key schedule and wrote the result back over the live keys. No safety
   check stands in front of an untagged union in any build mode. RFC 8446
   section 4.6.3 gives `key_update` to TLS 1.3 alone.

   The same arm reads `handshake[0]`, the `request_update` octet that says
   whether the write keys rotate too, and a `handshake_len` of zero passed
   the bound above it and left that slice empty.

   The loop itself read all four octets of a sub-message header before it
   checked that four octets were there, so an inner plaintext of one to
   four octets read the three octet length out of the record before this
   one, which the same peer wrote.

23. `verifyCertificateStrength`, in the appended block, called once for
   every certificate of both walks. `verifyEndEntity` reads the leaf and
   `verifyIssued` reads a pair, so the certificate at the point where the
   trust store answers passes through neither one as the subject, and its
   signature is the one the root made. The test *"both certificate walks
   call the one chain rule"* counts the call text of all three.

24. Two rules in `certificate.zig`, which is the bounded walk of item 20.

   The subject alternative names now come from 2.5.29.17 and from nothing
   else. `std.crypto.Certificate.ExtensionId` maps 2.5.29.7, the retired
   X.509(1988) spelling that no authority profiles or validates, to the
   same value, and the standard walk let the last of the two win. A
   certificate whose 2.5.29.17 named what the authority signed for,
   followed by a 2.5.29.7 naming something else, sent the host check to the
   second block. A second instance of 2.5.29.17 is refused as well: RFC
   5280 section 4.2 forbids it, and a walk that reads one of the two is
   guessing.

   The `signature` field of the `tbsCertificate` and the outer
   `signatureAlgorithm` field are now compared, whole DER against whole
   DER, which RFC 5280 section 4.1.1.2 asks for and which OpenSSL's
   `X509_ALGOR_cmp` does. The signature covers the inner field and the
   outer field picks the verifier, so two different names hand the verifier
   an algorithm the signer never signed.

What the patch does not change
------------------------------
The patch adds no CBC suite. The record cipher for a CBC suite is in
`tls.CipherSuite.With`, but the TLS 1.2 arm of this client builds only an AEAD
record cipher. A CBC suite would need much more than a name in a list, and
every server that matters offers a GCM or a ChaCha20 suite.

The patch adds no P-521. `Certificate.NamedCurve.Curve` refuses that curve at
compile time, so `verifyEcdsa` reports `TlsBadSignatureScheme` for it, which
is what upstream does as well.

The patch does not make TLS 1.3 lenient. `verifyEcdsa` takes the curve from
the certificate only for TLS 1.2. Over TLS 1.3 it still refuses a scheme name
whose curve is not the curve of the key.

The patch speaks no HTTP/2 itself. It offers the protocol names the caller
gives it and it reports the one the peer chose. `zurl-net/Connection.zig`
gives it `h2` and then `http/1.1`, and `zurl-http/h2.zig` is the engine that
reads the answer.

The patch does not choose a trust store. The host check of item 13 answers
one question, which is whether the leaf names the host that was asked for.
`init` runs the chain check after it, through `Options.ca`.

The patch adds no certificate policy check. RFC 5280 section 4.2.1.11
matters and it is not here. A `policyConstraints` extension is always
critical, so a certificate that carries one is refused by item 17 rather
than accepted with the constraint dropped, which is the closed answer.
`verifyIssued` is where the check goes, and adding it there reaches both
transports.

The name constraint check reads the host of the session and not every name
of the leaf. A name in the leaf that the constraint forbids and that
nobody asked for authenticates nothing, and the record buffer holds two
certificates at a time, so a copy of the leaf's names would have to be
made. RFC 5280 section 6.1.4 (g) asks for every name; this reads the one
that decides the session.

The TLS 1.2 arm of the `key_update` guard has no test of its own. The
fixture in `test_server.zig` speaks TLS 1.3 only, and a second fixture for
one line is not worth its weight. The guard reads `c.tls_version`, which
`init` sets from the Server Hello, and the TLS 1.3 arm of the same guard is
driven by `Options.fault.empty_key_update`.

The patch does not decide whether an HTTPS transfer detects truncation.
`allow_truncation_attacks` is a field of `Options` here and `zurl-http/h1.zig`
sets it, unconditionally, for every HTTPS transfer. The reason it gives is
that HTTP carries its own length, which holds for `Content-Length` and for
chunked framing and not for the third framing RFC 9112 section 6.3 still
permits, where the body ends at the end of the stream. For that framing
`close_notify` is the only thing that tells a whole body from a cut one, and
this file no longer reports one. The fix belongs in the engine that knows
the framing, not here.
